\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Alexander sent the hover-copt back to the city and rode shotgun in the lead truck. They\'92d only negotiated the bridge, the roundabout and were halfway down the mall when he observed, \'93There are a lot of Elvi in town.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Elvi had filled caf\'e9 outdoor areas and were gorging on multi-course breakfasts. Guitar cases leaned against tables. One was carrying a small amplifier out of a shop that normally sold lamps. Buskers performed outside every third shopfront window. On the corner a group of skateboard kids were arguing over who had discovered rock and roll. Though Alexander couldn\'92t hear the conversation, one of the kids said, \'93Doesn\'92t matter who invented it, my dad owns it,\'94 and went on to explain that music was just an undeveloped resource that had no intrinsic value until an agent or manager or publishing house came along and figured out how to sell it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 A promotional minibus and a ute loaded with cardboard boxes peeled off form the back of the convoy, parked and turned their hazard lights on, with no concern for blocking access to the supermarket carpark shortly before lunch on Christmas Eve.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 For the record, the supermarket was a Noworths, affectionately known as \'93The Flesh Wound People\'94, because no one escaped through the checkout without losing some skin.\
No one on the mall or in the developing traffic jam could have mistaken whose guerrilla brand activation it was. Alexander\'92s face was sprayed over the panels and windows of one side, vivid and alive, smiling like a politician who already knew how everyone had voted. The Wheel of Death filled the rear quarter panels in carnival colours. On the sliding-door side, most of the space  shouted:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\sb220\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 CANDID CRIMINAL\'97CHRISTMAS SPECIAL\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf2 YOU VOTE. THEY SPIN.
\fs22 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Snow fell on the lettering. A pair of the show\'92s skull logos wore Santa hats. A wreath of holly surrounded a smaller Wheel of Death, and Christmas baubles hung from lengths of rope. Candy canes crossed behind a judge\'92s gavel like cutlasses.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The minibus\'92s roof unfolded and speakers rose. The promotional team swarmed out, one carrying a t-shirt gun.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93Candid Criminal tonight live!\'94 the team captain announced through her headset microphone. \'93Are you ready to spin?\'94\
Another lowered the ute\'92s tail gate, leapt up and cut boxes open and tossed free merch to the rest of the crew, and any of the tightening crowd that waved for it. The speakers played the Candid Criminal theme tune arranged like Jingle Bells, as t-shirts were rolled around free show tickets and loaded into the cannon and shot above the crowd and honking traffic.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The crew grabbed merch by the armful and took it to the crowd: black caps embroidered with 
\fs20 WHEEL OF DEATH\'97EVERYBODY GETS A TURN
\fs22 ; T-shirts printed with 
\fs20 I CAME, I SAW, I VOTED GUILTY
\fs22  or 
\fs20 SPIN RESPONSIBLY
\fs22  or 
\fs20 MY OTHER SENTENCE IS DEATH.\

\fs22 Members of the crew patrolled both sides of the mall handing out free tickets to the show. Shop assistants appeared under awnings. A caf\'e9 lost three tables. Some shoppers who took them were more interested in seeing the famous Shakti mansion from close-up. A woman turned down the free merch; her husband grabbed one of everything.
\fs20  
\fs22 Some Airborne kids collected shirts, caps and tickets, then returned for more using accents they had not possessed only minutes earlier. \
One of the crew got struck at the knee and went down in a billow of tickets. Above him\'85 a woman who must have been eighty and, who was probably no taller than his shoulder\'97if he\'92d been on his feet\'97wearing a floral dress, boots and a cardigan buttoned all the way to the neck, and armed with a shopping trolley and a metal cane.\
\'93Move your taxing bus,\'94 she hissed and pushed her trolley on.\
A driver trapped behind the minibus wound down his window and cheered. A crew member offered her a cap; she put it on.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 UDF troops asked for tickets and were told to tax off, but not before being handed a t-shirt or a cap.
